From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Wed, 23 Mar 1994 22:55:43 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-266] Understanding Anarco-communists

There seem to be some misunderstandings of the anarco-communist critique of
extropianism that was forwarded here.  One is the claim that anarchism and
communism are mutually exclusive.  In theory, they can be compatible.  The
problem is that when it's put into practice, one part has to go: anarchy or
communism.  19th-century anarco-communists made many attempts to practice
their principles, and failed, leaving room for the statist communists to
take the lead in the 20th century.  The anarco-communist tradition has
remained, however, blaming the failures of State communism on the State,
and preserving faith in anarco-communism.  In Berkeley and San Francisco,
we have something of an anarco-communist community, which is why I've never
scared anyone off around here by telling them I'm an anarchist.

I've only gotten that reaction when I tell people I'm a capitalist, that I
believe in private property rights, that I'm against rent control, that I'm
against socialized medicine, that I'm against gun control, etc.  This is
the real difference between anarco-capitalist and anarco-socialism: the
theory of the function of private property, and economic theory.  Anarco-
socialists believe that the function of private property is to exploit, we
believe that its function is to protect.  Anarco-socialists believe that
capitalism is the institutionalization of this exploitation, we believe it
is the institutionalization of this protection.

Our critic was right, our differences are economic, but he never bothered to
explain his economic theory and defend it.  This is his weak point: he's
vulnerable to the socialist calculation impossibility proof here, as well
as questions of the origins of money and the theory of value.  If anyone
really wants to shut him down but quick, challenge him on these things.  If
you want to engage in an endless debate of ad hominems, mythologized and
demonized history, and other fallacies, try something else.

