I agree.  I also believe it's past time to be taking the next step,
which won't be the same for everyone b/c it depends on population
density, import/export needs, and desire for personal interaction.

One idea:

	Electric trolley lines, for instance, are practical in
high-density areas such as most major cities.  For towing cargo, one
could "check out" a handtruck at any collection point (most likely
where heavy stuff is sold) with an ATM card or similar, and check it
back in to any other collection point when done, getting your deposit
back modulo a small fee or credit for returning it to a place where it
is either surplus or needed badly.  Of course the trolleys have space
to easily hitch the hand trucks on or off at any stop.

	If these trolleys cover a sufficient area, the only traffic is
truck deliveries of huge objects and long-distance or rush trips.  The
money you save by not paying for a car or insurance can cover a LOT of
cab rides (which would be cheaper if the city didn't impose a fee
structure) and long-distance bus/train/plane trips.
	Plus, many blocks could be mostly torn up (since all that is
left is a train track and maybe a bike path or one lane of delivery
pavement) and replaced with parks, small marketplaces, or whatever,
since if you don't need to use your car much it's OK to walk a block
or two when you do; and given city parking now this is likely anyway.

	These lines would be private, since dispensation to build one
would be controlled by the property owners on that street, and if they
had any sense they'd keep it as a cooperative.  Same with the
hand trucks, etc.

	I'm not really interested in living in a city, but if you
desire cities this is one way to retrofit a solution to some noise, 
pollution, and gridlock problems.

