and our place on the political map:

>"Extropianism" appears to be built on top of the more
>reactionary/anti-enviromentalist aspects of the "Objectivist" and Capitalist
>"Libertarian" traditions in the United States, (and other industrial nations).

It's true that Extropianism derives in part from Objectivism, primarily its
emphasis on personal responsibility, self-sufficiency, and the need for
consistency in one's ethical code.  But most Extropians recognize the
serious flaws in Objectivism -- such as its naive-realist epistemology and
its dogmatism.  It's also true that Extropians are libertarians and
capitalists, but Mr. Hannah appears to believe that there is some tradition
of the ascendancy of these viewpoints in the U.S. and elsewhere.  In point
of fact, libertarianism and laissez-faire capitalism have never been the
dominant policy in any nation since the Industrial Revolution.

The dominant paradigm in the U.S., which Mr. Hannah mistakes for
capitalism, is in fact mercantilism -- the collusion of government with the
current giants of industry.  Mercantilism uses the power of the State to
ensure that "them as has, gets" -- enacting special legislation and
regulations to provide and perpetuate privileges for those presently in
power.  Some simple examples:  The FDA bans safe, effective, but
non-patentable remedies like L-tryptophan (an essential amino acid
necessary for health and a balanced diet, which is also effective as an
anti-depressant and nighttime sleep aid) so that pharmaceutical giants can
sell us expensive, patent-protected drugs like Prozac and Halcyon.  The FCC
regulates broadcast and cable television, ensuring that only State-approved
prolefeed reaches the people.  The State gives the AMA the power to
regulate and license the medical industry, ensuring a shortage of doctors,
a dearth of alternative therapies, and a continued oligopoly in medical
care.

True laissez-faire capitalism, taken to its extreme, is known as
propertarian anarchism or anarcho-capitalism.  We demand the expulsion of
the State from every facet of our lives, from the bedroom to the boardroom.
 "Keep your laws off my property" is our position, as is "Keep your laws
off my body".  The two statements are inseparable -- the regulation of
commerce necessarily means regulation of individual bodies.  The power to
ban any commercial product means the power to ban RU-486.  The power to
regulate all financial transactions means the power to regulate abortion. 
Ownership of all property by the State means State ownership of your flesh,
and we oppose it.  This is the position of Extropianism.

Mr. Hannah seems trapped in the "left-right" paradigm, a one-dimensional
view of politics which disguises the fact that there is another axis on the
map at right angles to the left-right axis: the statist vs. anti-statist
axis.  Both the conventional Left and the conventional Right want to
interfere with the natural workings of the market:  the Left, to ensure
greater equality of distribution; the Right, to ensure greater inequality
of distribution.  Both programs require a massive State, with a monopoly on
violence, to conduct the redistribution of wealth by force.

At the opposite end of the statist/anti-statist axis, anarchists -- both
anarcho-communists and anarcho-capitalists -- reject the State, with its
power to kill citizens who do not obey, and insist that all interactions be
voluntary.  Anarcho-syndicalists or anarcho-communists believe that, in the
absence of State coercion, voluntary transactions would lead to a
repudiation of property rights, resulting in complete material equality.

The anarcho-capitalist critique of this position is that property rights
are necessary to incentivize production.  Without ownership of property --
defined as the right to control, use, abuse, or destroy what is owned --
everything becomes what is called, in economics, a "public good".  A public
good is one that, if provided, benefits members of a group independently of
whether those members have paid for it.  Examples include national defense
and residential streets.  Consider the incentives of a potential
contributor:  "If I refuse to pay, the good may be provided anyway, and I
will have gotten it for free.  If I do pay, my contribution will be such a
small fraction of the total that I will not significantly increase the
amount of the good being provided, so I will refuse to pay."  (This is also
known as the free-rider problem, because some can get a free ride on the
efforts of others.)  The incentive structure is therefore such that public
goods -- without property rights, this means everything, from toothbrushes
to 747's -- are underproduced, from the standpoint of overall benefit.

