Mr. Hannah's affection for needless toil is certainly his business, but I
hope he doesn't succeed in forcing it on the rest of us.  Automation of
repetitive, demeaning tasks means that human beings can be freed to perform
creative, original, and intellectually-rewarding work.  I refer anyone who,
like Mr. Hannah, has a nostalgic affection for back-breaking labor, to such
classics as "The Right to be Lazy" by Paul LeFargue (the son-in-law of Karl
Marx) and "The Abolition of Work" by Bob Black (of the Last International).
 Both works recognize the potential of technology to free human beings from
drudgery and devote themselves to more enjoyable pursuits, although both
authors approach the subject from an anarcho-syndicalist, rather than an
anarcho-capitalist, point of view.

With regard to "becoming one with the machine," I refer interested readers
to the book "Mind Children" by Hans Moravec, Carnegie-Mellon robotics
researcher --  a classic Extropian text.

