On the contrary, Max More and the other founders of the Extropian movement
have taken great pains to try to avoid dogmatism and unfounded certainty. 
To quote from the Extropian Principles (Version 2.5):  "Extropians affirm
reason, critical inquiry, intellectual independence, and honesty.  We
reject blind faith and the passive, comfortable thinking that leads to
dogma, mysticism, and conformity.  Our commitment to positive
self-transformation requires us to critically analyze our current beliefs,
behaviors, and strategies.  Extropians therefore feel proud by readily
learning from error rather than by professing infallibility.  We prefer
analytical thought to fuzzy but comfortable delusion, empiricism to
mysticism, and independent evaluation to conformity."

The Extropian Principles is an essay by Max More (editor of Extropy and
President of Extropy Institute) that expounds the basic Extropian
world-view.  In keeping with our continued testing and revision of our
beliefs, each new edition of the Principles is version-numbered.

