>a feeling of emptiness, or general dissatisfaction with reality.)

We are opposed to all occurrence of human death, whether natural or
unnatural.  A man or woman killed by old age or anthrax is just as dead as
one killed in an industrial accident!  The number one factor that has
increased the human lifespan has been the acceleration of technology --
particularly medicine, agriculture, sanitation, and transportation
(improved distribution of food and medicine).  The more common causes of
death today -- cancer, cardiopulmonary disease -- were nearly unheard of in
the 16th century because they are diseases of *old age*.  The people of
earlier centuries were dying off at far younger ages, from starvation and
smallpox and dysentery.  The lengthening of lifespan -- the increased
abundance of human life -- is what has magnified the apparent occurrence of
the so-called "diseases of industrialized societies."  Life before industry
was indeed, as described by Hobbes, nasty, brutish, and, above all, short.

We stand today on the threshold of an unprecedented breakthrough -- the
elimination of aging and disease as causes of human death.  Extropians are
in favor of this breakthrough because we love life and want to keep living
it.  We value human intelligence -- individual human intelligence -- and
want to preserve and protect active, inquisitive minds.  The purpose of
technology is the improvement of human life, both in quantity and quality. 
I admit that I love indoor plumbing, jet travel, clean and durable
clothing, comfortable shelter, and cheap, abundant food.  If that makes me
a technocrat, I accept the label with pride.

Earlier, Mr. Hannah described Extropians as "anti-environmentalist".  I
must admit that this term is more precisely correct than Mr. Hannah appears
to mean it -- we are NOT "anti-environment," merely opposed to those rabid
*environmentalists* who wish to trample individual freedom and the progress
of human knowledge on the basis of inaccurate notions about the state of
the environment.  To quote again from the Principles:  "Resource limits are
not immutable.  Extropians affirm a rational, market-mediated
environmentalism aimed at sustaining and enhancing the conditions for our
flourishing.  We oppose 
apocalyptic environmentalism which hallucinates catastrophe, issues a
stream of irresponsible doomsday predictions, and attempts to strangle our
continued evolution.  Intelligent management of resources and environment
will be fostered by the Extropian goal of vastly extended lifespan.  The
market price system encourages conservation, substitution, and innovation,
preventing any need for 
a brake on growth and progress. Migration into space will immensely enlarge
the energy and resources accessible to our civilization.  Extended
lifespans will foster wisdom and foresight, while restraining recklessness
and profligacy."

Extropian hero Julian Simon has published a number of books demonstrating
the speciousness of the arguments of the apocalyptic environmentalists. 
(He won $10,000 in a bet with Paul Ehrlich about resource scarcity.) 
Open-minded readers are referred to Simon's books "Population Matters",
"The Ultimate Resource", and "The Resourceful Earth" (edited with Herman
Kahn).

